{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbXw2mATmKC46PQPoSaN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a repaired candidate, `length_load_encoding_noise`, and it passes the self-check. That check doesn't test novelty against the other models, so this candidate could still be rejected as a near-duplicate, and a second rejection is final. Its smoke fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −2307.1 (100 draws, one chain), close to the incumbent's −2301.4.\n\n**Why the first attempt was rejected:** it was the run-length variety idea, and its predictions matched `run_length_variety_preference` exactly (RMSE 0.00000). I couldn't switch to the other critique finding (people penalise very high alternation less than expected). Two existing models already cover it (`asymmetric_ideal_alternation_mirror` and `asymmetric_ideal_rate_decisiveness`), and three other candidates in this round propose the same thing.\n\n**New hypothesis:** this is a working-memory load account, as the brief asked for a process-level one. To compare two sequences people have to hold both in mind, and each extra flip adds noise. So the same difference in how random two sequences look decides the choice sharply for short sequences and only weakly for long ones. How random each sequence looks is still worked out exactly as in the incumbent (`individual_alternation_sensitivity_mirror`).\n\n**Code change:** I added one feature, the log of sequence length relative to 5. I also added one shared exponent, `eta`, with a Normal(0, 1) prior, that scales the score difference by (length/5)^(−eta). If `eta` comes out negative, longer sequences are judged more sharply instead.\n\n**Main risk:** if `eta` fits near zero, the model predicts almost exactly like the incumbent and fails the novelty check (RMSE must be at least 0.002).\n\nThe three files are in `iter_3/candidate_1_repair_1/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_rea